1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:08,000
1,500 miles, 11,000 soldiers, nearly 4,000 of them black,

2
00:00:08,100 --> 00:00:13,000
charged by a fateful time with a meaningful military role.

3
00:00:13,100 --> 00:00:14,000
Their task?

4
00:00:14,100 --> 00:00:17,000
Build a highway through some of the most imposing landscapes

5
00:00:17,100 --> 00:00:20,000
on Earth.

6
00:00:20,100 --> 00:00:24,000
Now, the Alcan Highway on Modern Marvels.

7
00:00:49,409 --> 00:00:54,200
A slender ribbon of two-lane highway transports more than 100,000 vacationers annually to

8
00:00:54,201 --> 00:00:59,940
Alaska and British Columbia. It was built around the Canadian

9
00:00:59,941 --> 00:01:05,000
Rockies during the region's most severe winter in history by men

10
00:01:05,001 --> 00:01:09,560
unfamiliar with the territory and the equipment.

11
00:01:09,561 --> 00:01:13,360
Born of necessity, some even saw the possibility that the fate of

12
00:01:13,361 --> 00:01:17,640
America could rely on its completion.

13
00:01:17,641 --> 00:01:22,560
1,500 miles long, so impressive that it's been officially

14
00:01:22,561 --> 00:01:25,860
recognized by the International Historical Engineering Landmark

15
00:01:25,861 --> 00:01:30,860
Commission as an engineering marvel.

16
00:01:30,861 --> 00:01:33,700
The Alaska Highway isn't a road like any other.

17
00:01:33,701 --> 00:01:35,300
It's really a piece of history.

18
00:01:35,301 --> 00:01:38,620
People come from all over the world to see it.

19
00:01:38,621 --> 00:01:41,820
After more than 60 years, the experience of building the

20
00:01:41,821 --> 00:01:45,560
Alcan Highway, now known as the Alaska Highway, is still

21
00:01:45,561 --> 00:01:48,360
unforgettable.

22
00:01:48,361 --> 00:01:52,520
The Alcan Highway was the greatest experience in my life.

23
00:01:52,521 --> 00:01:55,760
other than getting married.

24
00:01:55,761 --> 00:01:57,260
We didn't have no entertainment.

25
00:01:57,261 --> 00:01:59,620
We didn't have no liquor, thank goodness.

26
00:01:59,621 --> 00:02:03,760
We didn't have no, you know, supplied by the government.

27
00:02:03,761 --> 00:02:08,980
A terrain where you can look and see Mount Sanford

28
00:02:08,981 --> 00:02:12,800
50 miles away was really an experience for me,

29
00:02:12,801 --> 00:02:18,580
and I enjoyed the vastness and the beauty of the country.

30
00:02:18,581 --> 00:02:20,880
We knew we had a job to do.

31
00:02:20,881 --> 00:02:23,080
We did a beautiful job.

32
00:02:23,180 --> 00:02:25,920
And it's still a lasting experience

33
00:02:26,020 --> 00:02:30,460
that I never would have gotten had it not been for going to

34
00:02:30,560 --> 00:02:34,300
Alaska and World War II.

35
00:02:34,400 --> 00:02:37,120
Alaska's history, at least as far as the United States is

36
00:02:37,220 --> 00:02:41,740
concerned, starts in 1867.

37
00:02:41,840 --> 00:02:44,840
US Secretary of State William Seward signed an agreement with

38
00:02:44,940 --> 00:02:48,440
the Russian minister to the United States.

39
00:02:48,441 --> 00:02:53,280
The pact, jokingly known as Seward's Folly, ceded 586,000

40
00:02:53,281 --> 00:03:00,280
square miles of land to the US for a mere $7.2 million.

41
00:03:00,281 --> 00:03:03,280
The isolated region sat pristinely for the next 30

42
00:03:03,281 --> 00:03:07,800
years, totally ignored by Washington officials until an

43
00:03:07,801 --> 00:03:11,200
earth-shattering discovery was made.

44
00:03:11,201 --> 00:03:15,600
Gold. The 1897 Yukon Gold Rush put the

45
00:03:15,601 --> 00:03:21,600
region on the map. At its height, 50,000 men and

46
00:03:21,601 --> 00:03:26,600
women raced to Alaska looking to make their fortune.

47
00:03:26,601 --> 00:03:29,600
However, Alaska's claim as the gold capital of the world was

48
00:03:29,601 --> 00:03:33,600
short-lived. By 1900, as new gold claims

49
00:03:33,601 --> 00:03:39,600
dwindled, so did the gold seekers.

50
00:03:39,601 --> 00:03:43,600
But even without gold, Alaska was rich in other resources.

51
00:03:43,601 --> 00:03:48,600
mining, fishing, and trapping all flourished.

52
00:03:48,601 --> 00:03:51,600
This spurred members of Congress to confer official territory

53
00:03:51,601 --> 00:03:57,600
status in 1912. It would take 47 more years before

54
00:03:57,601 --> 00:04:04,600
Alaska would gain statehood. Originally called the Pioneer

55
00:04:04,601 --> 00:04:07,600
Road, the idea of a highway that would bridge the gap between

56
00:04:07,601 --> 00:04:10,600
the Lower 48 and the Alaskan wilderness had been around since

57
00:04:10,601 --> 00:04:14,600
been around since the 1860s.

58
00:04:14,700 --> 00:04:18,840
Finally, in 1938, a five-member US Alaskan Highway

59
00:04:18,940 --> 00:04:23,040
Commission was formed to explore the idea of building a road.

60
00:04:25,840 --> 00:04:29,280
Alaskans have been pushing for a road to the states,

61
00:04:29,380 --> 00:04:31,280
what they called the International Highway,

62
00:04:31,380 --> 00:04:33,160
since 1929.

63
00:04:33,260 --> 00:04:34,920
Between the US and Canadian governments,

64
00:04:35,020 --> 00:04:38,060
they surveyed and came up with two proposed lines,

65
00:04:38,061 --> 00:04:43,700
the A route, and the B route, which is farther to the east.

66
00:04:43,800 --> 00:04:47,000
Neither of these routes satisfied both governments.

67
00:04:47,100 --> 00:04:49,180
Additionally, the Canadians wanted the new highway

68
00:04:49,280 --> 00:04:51,740
to link a series of seven airfields

69
00:04:51,840 --> 00:04:54,240
that stretched from Alberta, Canada west to Alaska.

70
00:04:57,180 --> 00:05:00,620
The governments compromised and greenlighted a sea route,

71
00:05:00,720 --> 00:05:04,860
or prairie route, a highway that would link the series of airfields

72
00:05:04,861 --> 00:05:09,660
as the Northwest Staging Route. Sea Route, which would start in

73
00:05:09,661 --> 00:05:13,100
Dawson Creek, British Columbia and end in Fairbanks, Alaska,

74
00:05:13,101 --> 00:05:18,100
was approved for several strategic reasons.

75
00:05:18,101 --> 00:05:20,600
The most important reason was that this route was farther

76
00:05:20,601 --> 00:05:25,220
inland, and thus less vulnerable to attack.

77
00:05:25,221 --> 00:05:28,420
This was a concern because of Japan's increasingly aggressive

78
00:05:28,421 --> 00:05:33,220
behavior. It was also more calm in the

79
00:05:33,221 --> 00:05:36,920
the interior. It didn't have all the storms and climate

80
00:05:36,921 --> 00:05:39,460
problems that you would get on the ocean.

81
00:05:39,461 --> 00:05:42,460
And thirdly, because it was connected by roads to the

82
00:05:42,461 --> 00:05:45,460
industrial and commercial heartland in the United States

83
00:05:45,461 --> 00:05:49,900
and Canada. However,

84
00:05:49,901 --> 00:05:52,980
territorial bickering within the Canadian government pushed the

85
00:05:52,981 --> 00:05:55,380
proposed highway to the back burner.

86
00:05:58,380 --> 00:06:01,380
Escalating concerns in Europe would eventually play a role in

87
00:06:01,381 --> 00:06:06,880
in the highway's birth. By summer 1941, World War II

88
00:06:06,881 --> 00:06:11,380
was intensifying, and the US was getting more involved.

89
00:06:11,381 --> 00:06:14,880
But not officially at war, President Roosevelt redirected

90
00:06:14,881 --> 00:06:19,380
25% of the Pacific Fleet to the Atlantic.

91
00:06:19,381 --> 00:06:22,880
But the US didn't officially enter World War II until one

92
00:06:22,881 --> 00:06:30,380
infamous day. In the wake of the surprise

93
00:06:30,381 --> 00:06:33,020
of the surprise attack on Pearl Harbor,

94
00:06:33,021 --> 00:06:35,020
Japan was now a continuing threat

95
00:06:35,021 --> 00:06:38,020
to America's Pacific fleet and its shipping lanes

96
00:06:38,021 --> 00:06:39,620
throughout that region.

97
00:06:39,621 --> 00:06:42,200
If the Japanese could attack America's military bases

98
00:06:42,201 --> 00:06:44,800
in the middle of the ocean, what would prevent them

99
00:06:44,801 --> 00:06:50,440
from striking again, only this time on the mainland?

100
00:06:50,441 --> 00:06:54,300
No matter how long it may take us

101
00:06:54,301 --> 00:06:58,940
to overcome this premeditated invasion, the American...

102
00:06:58,941 --> 00:07:02,720
It is safe to say that the highway would have never been built without Pearl Harbor, or

103
00:07:02,721 --> 00:07:07,700
without the fear that Tony Diamond, who was Alaska's delegate to Congress, said that Alaska

104
00:07:07,701 --> 00:07:10,911
was going to become the second Pearl Harbor unless the U.S.

105
00:07:10,912 --> 00:07:12,380
government did something to

106
00:07:12,381 --> 00:07:14,420
fortify it very quickly.

107
00:07:14,421 --> 00:07:21,860
Throughout December 1941, there were 58 sightings of hostile ships or aircraft in

108
00:07:21,861 --> 00:07:24,780
the Gulf of Alaska.

109
00:07:24,781 --> 00:07:27,180
If the Japanese controlled the shipping lanes in the North

110
00:07:27,181 --> 00:07:31,020
Pacific, they could seize a foothold in Alaska and push

111
00:07:31,021 --> 00:07:33,420
south to the lower 48.

112
00:07:33,421 --> 00:07:39,720
We Americans are vitally concerned in your defense

113
00:07:39,721 --> 00:07:41,940
of freedom.

114
00:07:41,941 --> 00:07:47,040
FDR quickly approved the idea of building sea route.

115
00:07:47,041 --> 00:07:49,980
It would be built as fast as possible.

116
00:07:49,981 --> 00:07:52,680
The Canadians agreed, and the two countries divided

117
00:07:52,681 --> 00:07:58,680
for the highway's construction. The US Army Corps of Engineers

118
00:07:58,681 --> 00:08:01,680
would supply the bulk of the manpower.

119
00:08:01,681 --> 00:08:04,680
The war at that point was really escalating.

120
00:08:04,681 --> 00:08:07,680
People were frightened. We knew this was important.

121
00:08:07,681 --> 00:08:12,680
Alaska was threatened. All these bases were threatened.

122
00:08:12,681 --> 00:08:15,680
The Cabinet Committee appointed by President Roosevelt

123
00:08:15,681 --> 00:08:18,680
initially recommended sending four engineering regiments to

124
00:08:18,681 --> 00:08:26,320
to Canada and Alaska. Each regiment would have 1,300 men.

125
00:08:26,321 --> 00:08:30,120
Eventually, seven regiments, comprised of 11,000 soldiers

126
00:08:30,121 --> 00:08:33,520
and officers, would travel by rail and ship north to

127
00:08:33,521 --> 00:08:36,640
territories where the wildlife population dwarfed the human

128
00:08:36,641 --> 00:08:41,640
population. With troops already thinly stretched

129
00:08:41,641 --> 00:08:45,640
across the Pacific and Atlantic, the pool of potential regiments

130
00:08:45,641 --> 00:08:50,640
to head north would have to be widened.

131
00:08:50,740 --> 00:08:53,380
If FDR wanted to build this highway quickly,

132
00:08:53,480 --> 00:08:55,080
he had to make an unpopular decision.

133
00:08:58,980 --> 00:09:01,960
Roosevelt assigned three all-black engineer regiments

134
00:09:02,060 --> 00:09:06,760
to Alaska against the wishes of his Alaskan commander,

135
00:09:06,860 --> 00:09:10,640
Simon Bolivar Buckner, Jr.

136
00:09:10,740 --> 00:09:13,360
It so happens that the commander in Alaska

137
00:09:13,361 --> 00:09:18,760
was from the deep south and did not want black troops.

138
00:09:18,860 --> 00:09:21,860
And he protested roundly.

139
00:09:21,960 --> 00:09:25,640
And in order to placate him, the people who sent the troops

140
00:09:25,740 --> 00:09:27,940
from Washington said they would not

141
00:09:28,040 --> 00:09:31,480
put the black troops near any towns, no villages.

142
00:09:31,580 --> 00:09:35,120
He had said that if they came into the Alaskan towns

143
00:09:35,220 --> 00:09:37,460
and villages, they would interbreed

144
00:09:37,560 --> 00:09:39,320
with the native Indians and Eskimos

145
00:09:39,420 --> 00:09:41,920
and produce the worst looking race in the history of the world.

146
00:09:41,921 --> 00:09:44,120
and he wouldn't have it.

147
00:09:44,121 --> 00:09:46,220
The black troops during World War II

148
00:09:46,221 --> 00:09:49,620
had, until this point, just been doing housekeeping duty.

149
00:09:49,621 --> 00:09:51,860
They'd been manning stateside bases.

150
00:09:51,861 --> 00:09:56,240
They'd not been given any real responsible jobs.

151
00:09:56,241 --> 00:10:00,800
Black soldiers were given a rare, significant military task.

152
00:10:00,801 --> 00:10:02,640
They would build the highway that would thwart

153
00:10:02,641 --> 00:10:06,140
the Japanese invasion.

154
00:10:06,141 --> 00:10:09,040
The few residents of towns where the highway was headed

155
00:10:09,041 --> 00:10:12,040
to be on the front lines of a social experiment.

156
00:10:14,940 --> 00:10:19,840
In February 1942, approximately 4,000 black soldiers traveled

157
00:10:19,841 --> 00:10:23,880
by rail to an unknown destination.

158
00:10:23,881 --> 00:10:25,600
We don't know where we are going.

159
00:10:25,601 --> 00:10:27,900
We will never get back home.

160
00:10:27,901 --> 00:10:30,900
That was the big thing.

161
00:10:30,901 --> 00:10:32,600
But it wasn't only black soldiers

162
00:10:32,601 --> 00:10:36,440
who were facing the bitter cold and an unknown assignment.

163
00:10:36,441 --> 00:10:39,000
Some white soldiers thought they were headed for Hawaii,

164
00:10:39,001 --> 00:10:42,300
beaches. I was out in Fort

165
00:10:42,301 --> 00:10:46,240
Leonard Wood, Missouri, and I was shipped to Fort Ord,

166
00:10:46,241 --> 00:10:49,540
California, where we were supposed to ship out for the

167
00:10:49,541 --> 00:10:51,980
islands. They'd taken our gun and rifles

168
00:10:51,981 --> 00:10:55,760
away from us and give us winter clothes, sleeping bags.

169
00:10:55,761 --> 00:10:59,820
It was for 40 degree weather. We knew that something was up.

170
00:11:02,280 --> 00:11:06,760
By March 1942, thousands of American GIs were riding the

171
00:11:06,761 --> 00:11:09,760
on their way to Alaska and Canada, still not certain of

172
00:11:09,761 --> 00:11:13,760
their mission or even where they were headed.

173
00:11:13,761 --> 00:11:19,760
In 1942, Dawson Creek, British Columbia was a sleepy farming

174
00:11:19,761 --> 00:11:23,760
village with a population of 600 hard-working, God-fearing

175
00:11:23,761 --> 00:11:27,760
Canadians. That winter, residents were

176
00:11:27,761 --> 00:11:31,760
preparing for an invasion, not of Japanese soldiers, but

177
00:11:31,761 --> 00:11:38,760
but American GIs. On March 9, the first of 11,000

178
00:11:38,761 --> 00:11:42,760
troops, including 4,000 black soldiers, emptied out of trains

179
00:11:42,761 --> 00:11:45,760
at Dawson Creek. The rumor got around that

180
00:11:45,761 --> 00:11:48,760
they were coming, and so we all expected them, but nothing like

181
00:11:48,761 --> 00:11:54,760
what we did get. Boy, there's just trainfuls

182
00:11:54,761 --> 00:12:00,760
and trainfuls. U.S. military officials

183
00:12:00,761 --> 00:12:03,760
officials had decided to use a three-prong attack to build the

184
00:12:03,761 --> 00:12:08,760
highway as quickly as possible. The Dawson Creek-based

185
00:12:08,761 --> 00:12:11,760
regiments would work north to the Yukon border.

186
00:12:11,761 --> 00:12:15,760
The regiments in the Yukon would work south to Dawson Creek and

187
00:12:15,761 --> 00:12:19,760
north to Alaska. The last regiment would head south

188
00:12:19,761 --> 00:12:25,760
from Alaska to the Yukon. The soldiers were stunned by the

189
00:12:25,761 --> 00:12:28,760
freezing weather. The locals were shocked at the sight

190
00:12:28,761 --> 00:12:31,760
of so many soldiers.

191
00:12:31,860 --> 00:12:34,460
Regiments themselves were bigger than any established

192
00:12:34,560 --> 00:12:38,200
towns and communities in the North at that time.

193
00:12:38,300 --> 00:12:41,500
They had seven regiments of US Army engineers,

194
00:12:41,600 --> 00:12:42,700
and they spread out.

195
00:12:42,800 --> 00:12:44,400
They went here and there.

196
00:12:44,500 --> 00:12:47,820
Whitehorse then was a population of about 450 people

197
00:12:47,920 --> 00:12:48,820
year round.

198
00:12:48,920 --> 00:12:51,520
All of a sudden, in about three or four months,

199
00:12:51,620 --> 00:12:54,120
there were 5,000 people living in Whitehorse,

200
00:12:54,220 --> 00:12:56,420
and most of it was US Army personnel.

201
00:12:58,760 --> 00:13:01,660
More than one-third of the soldiers descending on the Yukon,

202
00:13:01,760 --> 00:13:05,360
Canada, and Alaska were black.

203
00:13:05,460 --> 00:13:08,200
Many of these men were from the Deep South

204
00:13:08,300 --> 00:13:10,000
and poorly educated.

205
00:13:10,100 --> 00:13:12,300
We all worked together.

206
00:13:12,400 --> 00:13:15,000
If you couldn't read or write, we would write letters home

207
00:13:15,100 --> 00:13:17,520
for the illiterate ones.

208
00:13:17,620 --> 00:13:20,020
And we even taught some of the illiterate ones

209
00:13:20,120 --> 00:13:23,120
to read and write.

210
00:13:23,220 --> 00:13:27,160
Illiterate or educated, this was still the US Army.

211
00:13:27,161 --> 00:13:30,560
And all black soldiers were still subjected to Jim Crow laws,

212
00:13:30,561 --> 00:13:33,960
a system of racial segregation.

213
00:13:33,961 --> 00:13:36,800
Following Alaskan Commander Buckner's orders,

214
00:13:36,801 --> 00:13:40,040
the all black 97th Regiment was sent deep into the Alaskan

215
00:13:40,041 --> 00:13:43,920
wilderness, away from everyone.

216
00:13:43,921 --> 00:13:48,320
We had no facility to take showers and anything like that.

217
00:13:48,321 --> 00:13:52,320
It was a question of heating water in your helmet and taking

218
00:13:52,321 --> 00:13:56,120
a little bird bath. Supply truck sightings were

219
00:13:56,121 --> 00:14:00,620
for the 97th. But conditions weren't much

220
00:14:00,621 --> 00:14:04,000
better for the white troops. Often supplies couldn't reach

221
00:14:04,001 --> 00:14:08,600
them either because of bad weather or unfinished roads.

222
00:14:08,601 --> 00:14:11,360
Also, the best clothing the military could offer wasn't

223
00:14:11,361 --> 00:14:15,400
enough to keep all the soldiers warm against the Arctic weather.

224
00:14:15,401 --> 00:14:19,440
In spite of having lived in 72 degrees below zero,

225
00:14:19,441 --> 00:14:25,040
we only had frozen fingers, frozen ears, or maybe

226
00:14:25,041 --> 00:14:30,620
maybe the tissue on the face were burned off.

227
00:14:30,720 --> 00:14:35,320
Many soldiers suffered from frostbite.

228
00:14:35,420 --> 00:14:37,520
Frostbite is often the freezing of the skin,

229
00:14:37,620 --> 00:14:41,760
usually in the extremities. In severe cases, muscle and even

230
00:14:41,860 --> 00:14:48,160
bone can freeze. If cold enough, frostbite can

231
00:14:48,260 --> 00:14:52,460
happen in just a few minutes, with deadly results.

232
00:14:52,461 --> 00:14:55,100
more than 50 soldiers lost their lives to the frigid

233
00:14:55,200 --> 00:14:57,200
conditions.

234
00:14:57,300 --> 00:15:00,600
The battle against temperatures of 70 degrees below zero,

235
00:15:00,700 --> 00:15:03,340
and clothing designed for 40 degrees above,

236
00:15:03,440 --> 00:15:07,480
can only last so long.

237
00:15:07,580 --> 00:15:11,040
One soldier from the 97th died from exposure walking back to

238
00:15:11,140 --> 00:15:14,880
camp after his truck broke down.

239
00:15:14,980 --> 00:15:19,080
After his death, the idea came up there every 10 miles,

240
00:15:19,081 --> 00:15:23,080
a little shack with a stove was put along the road.

241
00:15:23,081 --> 00:15:26,520
So in case you got stuck on the road, you never had over five

242
00:15:26,521 --> 00:15:31,320
miles to walk to some type of shelter.

243
00:15:31,321 --> 00:15:33,720
Black and white soldiers endured the same living

244
00:15:33,721 --> 00:15:36,840
conditions. They lived in six-man pyramid

245
00:15:36,841 --> 00:15:39,800
tents, which limited their exposure to the elements, but

246
00:15:39,801 --> 00:15:42,360
were no match against the sub-freezing temperatures.

247
00:15:45,480 --> 00:15:48,920
In search of warmer clothing, one black soldier surprised and

248
00:15:48,921 --> 00:15:50,580
an Indian village.

249
00:15:50,581 --> 00:15:54,360
The command in Alaska said they should not be allowed

250
00:15:54,361 --> 00:15:57,260
near any Alaskan towns or villages.

251
00:15:57,261 --> 00:15:59,760
There is one case, though, that I really love.

252
00:15:59,761 --> 00:16:02,960
The black bulldozer driver and his crew

253
00:16:02,961 --> 00:16:07,380
went through a very small Canadian Indian village.

254
00:16:07,381 --> 00:16:11,680
And a researcher went through about 10 years later

255
00:16:11,681 --> 00:16:14,820
and asked about the first time they'd actually

256
00:16:14,821 --> 00:16:17,020
seen the Alcan Highway builders.

257
00:16:17,021 --> 00:16:19,260
And one of the Indians said, you know,

258
00:16:19,261 --> 00:16:21,960
the first white person I ever saw was black.

259
00:16:25,920 --> 00:16:27,640
Arriving with the troops by train

260
00:16:27,641 --> 00:16:31,860
were thousands of bulldozers, tractors, and graders.

261
00:16:31,861 --> 00:16:34,840
The mission was to knock down spruce and pine trees,

262
00:16:34,841 --> 00:16:37,640
lay an 18-foot-wide gravel road, wide enough

263
00:16:37,641 --> 00:16:40,580
for two military trucks, smooth out the gravel,

264
00:16:40,581 --> 00:16:46,480
and repeat for more than 1,600 miles.

265
00:16:46,481 --> 00:16:49,980
equivalent to that from Washington, D.C., to Denver,

266
00:16:49,981 --> 00:16:53,980
Colorado. To supply gasoline and other

267
00:16:53,981 --> 00:16:57,320
petroleum products to the regiments, the U.S. and Canadian

268
00:16:57,321 --> 00:17:02,820
governments created CANOL. CANOL would consist of four

269
00:17:02,821 --> 00:17:07,200
components, a producing oil field, a pipeline to carry crude

270
00:17:07,201 --> 00:17:13,100
oil to Whitehorse, a refinery, and finally, another 1,000 mile

271
00:17:13,101 --> 00:17:17,300
system of pipelines to deliver refined gasoline to the airfields

272
00:17:17,301 --> 00:17:19,500
along the Northwest staging route.

273
00:17:22,340 --> 00:17:24,500
Kano would be built concurrent with the highway.

274
00:17:27,440 --> 00:17:30,400
As the Kano project began, soldiers started getting

275
00:17:30,401 --> 00:17:32,900
acquainted with equipment few had ever used.

276
00:17:36,400 --> 00:17:39,400
These regiments were made up of combat engineers, trained more

277
00:17:39,401 --> 00:17:41,400
for warfare than road building.

278
00:17:43,100 --> 00:17:47,100
Most regiments would have 20 of the large D-8 tractors,

279
00:17:47,200 --> 00:17:51,740
24 small D-4 tractors, 50 to 90 dump trucks,

280
00:17:51,840 --> 00:17:53,740
and an assortment of Jeeps.

281
00:17:53,840 --> 00:17:56,440
They weren't the quietest trucks in the world,

282
00:17:56,540 --> 00:17:58,440
and they made an awful noise.

283
00:17:58,540 --> 00:18:01,360
If a convoy went by, you had to holler at each other

284
00:18:01,460 --> 00:18:04,360
so you could hear, because it went whooping by,

285
00:18:04,460 --> 00:18:06,900
and everything settled down, and pretty soon here

286
00:18:07,000 --> 00:18:08,400
comes another one.

287
00:18:08,500 --> 00:18:11,800
And this was the way it went.

288
00:18:11,801 --> 00:18:15,040
The first few days in camp set the tone for the grueling work

289
00:18:15,140 --> 00:18:16,340
facing all the soldiers.

290
00:18:18,540 --> 00:18:21,540
First thing you done, you grabbed yourself a cup of coffee.

291
00:18:21,640 --> 00:18:25,440
We'd get that down us, and then we'd have our breakfast,

292
00:18:25,540 --> 00:18:29,960
whatever it was, and hit the road.

293
00:18:30,060 --> 00:18:33,160
Hitting the road meant back-breaking 12 to 14-hour

294
00:18:33,260 --> 00:18:36,360
days, seven days a week.

295
00:18:36,460 --> 00:18:37,900
They learned by doing.

296
00:18:38,000 --> 00:18:41,800
You didn't shut off a car or a truck or a bus.

297
00:18:41,801 --> 00:18:44,800
You kept them running 24 hours a day.

298
00:18:44,801 --> 00:18:46,800
When it's 30 and 40 below zero,

299
00:18:46,801 --> 00:18:47,800
metal becomes very brittle.

300
00:18:47,801 --> 00:18:49,800
It breaks very easily.

301
00:18:49,801 --> 00:18:53,800
The first tractors we got were not made for that kind of work.

302
00:18:53,801 --> 00:18:56,800
They were D4s and R4s,

303
00:18:56,801 --> 00:18:58,800
and we'd still be building the road

304
00:18:58,801 --> 00:19:00,800
if we'd stayed with that equipment.

305
00:19:00,801 --> 00:19:02,800
But then when they come in there

306
00:19:02,801 --> 00:19:08,800
with the big D8 diesel tractors, we went to work.

307
00:19:08,801 --> 00:19:10,800
I never seen a D8 before.

308
00:19:10,801 --> 00:19:14,400
My God, that was a monster when I crawled up on that thing.

309
00:19:14,401 --> 00:19:16,200
But it didn't take me long to learn.

310
00:19:19,200 --> 00:19:23,340
Using 17,000-ton caterpillar tractors and bulldozers,

311
00:19:23,341 --> 00:19:25,880
the troops had difficulty controlling their equipment

312
00:19:25,881 --> 00:19:27,180
because of the rough terrain.

313
00:19:29,320 --> 00:19:33,080
The D8s were the world's largest tractors at that time,

314
00:19:33,081 --> 00:19:36,500
more than 15 feet long, 7 1⁄2 feet high,

315
00:19:36,501 --> 00:19:38,000
and almost 9 feet wide.

316
00:19:38,001 --> 00:19:43,000
wide. Running four or five abreast,

317
00:19:43,001 --> 00:19:48,000
the bulldozers knocked down and uprooted trees, cutting a 100-foot

318
00:19:48,001 --> 00:19:53,000
wide swath. The second wave of bulldozers and

319
00:19:53,001 --> 00:19:57,000
tractors pushed the debris to the side.

320
00:19:57,001 --> 00:20:01,000
Following them was a regimental company that smoothed and graded

321
00:20:01,001 --> 00:20:05,000
the roads. It was known as the train system,

322
00:20:05,001 --> 00:20:10,000
All units moving forward, doing their job over and over again.

323
00:20:10,100 --> 00:20:14,540
The situation in which the soldiers found themselves

324
00:20:14,640 --> 00:20:16,440
was in a total wilderness.

325
00:20:16,540 --> 00:20:18,540
It's a really tough country.

326
00:20:18,640 --> 00:20:22,660
They were building five miles of road, moving on.

327
00:20:22,760 --> 00:20:26,760
They were going into forests that had never been cut.

328
00:20:26,860 --> 00:20:30,100
They were going in mountains that had seldom been climbed.

329
00:20:30,200 --> 00:20:35,000
They were going into country that hadn't even been mapped.

330
00:20:35,001 --> 00:20:38,800
Led by local surveyors and trappers, the soldiers blazed

331
00:20:38,801 --> 00:20:41,800
trails over pristine territory.

332
00:20:41,801 --> 00:20:44,800
But as spring melted away winter's problems,

333
00:20:44,801 --> 00:20:48,240
it revealed new obstacles that could severely delay the

334
00:20:48,241 --> 00:20:50,240
highway's construction.

335
00:20:53,760 --> 00:20:56,560
May 1942.

336
00:20:56,561 --> 00:20:59,800
11,000 soldiers continued their push through the wilderness of

337
00:20:59,801 --> 00:21:05,000
British Columbia, Alaska, and the Yukon Territory.

338
00:21:05,001 --> 00:21:12,940
Four white U.S. combat engineer regiments armed with massive tractors moved quickly

339
00:21:12,941 --> 00:21:16,920
through the brush, cutting and grading about five miles a day.

340
00:21:19,140 --> 00:21:22,020
However, their black counterparts weren't as well equipped.

341
00:21:23,020 --> 00:21:24,505
They got the lousy jobs.

342
00:21:24,506 --> 00:21:28,340
They got to work with hand tools, whereas, you know, the white

343
00:21:28,341 --> 00:21:30,600
soldiers were driving trucks and driving bulldozers.

344
00:21:30,601 --> 00:21:34,840
There was a theory before these troops were sent north

345
00:21:34,841 --> 00:21:37,540
that black people didn't have the intelligence

346
00:21:37,541 --> 00:21:39,380
to operate heavy equipment.

347
00:21:39,900 --> 00:21:43,740
The leaders of the black troops quickly discovered

348
00:21:43,741 --> 00:21:47,380
that these guys were very good at operating heavy equipment,

349
00:21:48,220 --> 00:21:50,620
but equipment was limited.

350
00:21:50,880 --> 00:21:53,980
Sometimes the white troops would grab it,

351
00:21:54,520 --> 00:21:57,500
and so they were left with picks and shovels.

352
00:21:57,760 --> 00:22:00,340
But they were very good with those picks and shovels,

353
00:22:00,341 --> 00:22:04,140
and they were very motivated to do a good job.

354
00:22:06,480 --> 00:22:09,420
While the black soldiers were treated mostly even-handedly

355
00:22:09,421 --> 00:22:12,700
by their commanding officers, the lack of proper equipment

356
00:22:12,701 --> 00:22:16,600
forced the 97th combat engineers to take matters

357
00:22:16,601 --> 00:22:17,620
into their own hands.

358
00:22:19,760 --> 00:22:23,380
They used to, once in a while, go over to do a midnight

359
00:22:23,381 --> 00:22:26,500
requisition and steal a bulldozer or something

360
00:22:26,501 --> 00:22:30,980
from the white camp and build a big hunk of road with it

361
00:22:30,981 --> 00:22:32,880
and then put out a sign that said,

362
00:22:32,881 --> 00:22:35,380
this was built by the 97th.

363
00:22:35,381 --> 00:22:37,620
Imagine what we could do if we had equipment.

364
00:22:40,580 --> 00:22:42,920
Regardless of equipment or supplies,

365
00:22:42,921 --> 00:22:45,520
there were two natural problems that paralyzed those big

366
00:22:45,521 --> 00:22:47,620
tractors and bulldozers.

367
00:22:47,621 --> 00:22:51,220
Muskeg and permafrost jeopardized the entire project.

368
00:22:51,221 --> 00:22:56,460
Muskeg is a peat bog that freezes in the winter.

369
00:22:56,560 --> 00:23:01,000
It's made up of water, mosses, vegetable matter, and soil,

370
00:23:01,100 --> 00:23:03,460
and is only found in the Arctic.

371
00:23:03,560 --> 00:23:06,900
Muskeg can reach depths of 50 feet below the surface.

372
00:23:10,040 --> 00:23:12,380
When soldiers first discovered Muskeg,

373
00:23:12,480 --> 00:23:15,320
they scraped off its top layer and pushed it to the side

374
00:23:15,420 --> 00:23:16,480
of the road.

375
00:23:16,580 --> 00:23:18,240
That was a mistake.

376
00:23:18,340 --> 00:23:20,040
You know what a swamp looks like?

377
00:23:20,041 --> 00:23:22,940
Well, these could bury a D8 tractor.

378
00:23:23,040 --> 00:23:25,940
As long as you kept moving, you were all right.

379
00:23:26,040 --> 00:23:28,140
If you ever stopped, you went down.

380
00:23:28,240 --> 00:23:32,280
And to get you out of there took a lot of work.

381
00:23:32,380 --> 00:23:34,480
As the spring turned into summer,

382
00:23:34,580 --> 00:23:36,560
tractors on the cleared muskeg swamps

383
00:23:36,660 --> 00:23:40,160
were being swallowed up by mud on a regular basis.

384
00:23:40,260 --> 00:23:44,100
When you get the tractor stuck in the mud up there,

385
00:23:44,200 --> 00:23:48,160
if you leave it running, the doggone thing vibrate,

386
00:23:48,161 --> 00:23:50,800
vibrating, it just keep vibrating down until all it'd

387
00:23:50,801 --> 00:23:56,100
be sticking out was the stack, the exhaust pipe.

388
00:23:56,101 --> 00:24:00,700
We finally conquered the mud and managed to keep on pumping.

389
00:24:00,701 --> 00:24:05,040
We just kept going. After several tractors were

390
00:24:05,041 --> 00:24:08,580
disabled by the mud, the troops finally realized it was better

391
00:24:08,581 --> 00:24:14,220
to leave all Muskeg in place. An old construction method was

392
00:24:14,221 --> 00:24:20,220
on site to traverse the muskeg. Soldiers would build corduroy

393
00:24:20,221 --> 00:24:23,060
road from fallen trees. More trees would be laid

394
00:24:23,061 --> 00:24:26,260
perpendicular to the road. The soldiers would then place a

395
00:24:26,261 --> 00:24:29,760
mesh of branches over the logs, and finally soil would be poured

396
00:24:29,761 --> 00:24:36,380
on top of the branches to create the road surface.

397
00:24:36,381 --> 00:24:38,780
Permafrost was the second problem the soldiers had to

398
00:24:38,781 --> 00:24:43,480
overcome. Permafrost is ground that's been

399
00:24:43,481 --> 00:24:48,480
for at least two years. Soldiers would park their

400
00:24:48,481 --> 00:24:52,480
equipment on permafrost, only to see the heavy machinery sink

401
00:24:52,481 --> 00:24:57,480
deep into the mud, thawed by the tractor's engine heat.

402
00:24:57,481 --> 00:25:02,480
Permafrost features are varied. From the air, polygonal designs

403
00:25:02,481 --> 00:25:06,480
in the ground are visible. From the ground, stunted black

404
00:25:06,481 --> 00:25:12,480
spruce trees are apparent. Using aerial surveillance to

405
00:25:12,481 --> 00:25:15,980
to determine permafrost areas, military officials finally

406
00:25:15,981 --> 00:25:20,580
decided to build around it as best they could.

407
00:25:20,581 --> 00:25:23,920
The GIs were not only fighting muskeg and permafrost,

408
00:25:23,921 --> 00:25:27,720
but millions of mosquitoes. Due to a major oversight

409
00:25:27,721 --> 00:25:30,560
by military leaders, unlike the soldiers in Europe

410
00:25:30,561 --> 00:25:34,800
and in the Pacific, they received no bug repellent.

411
00:25:34,801 --> 00:25:40,040
We worked on the World War I campaign hats covered with nets.

412
00:25:40,041 --> 00:25:43,880
We slept on the mosquito bars over our little cots,

413
00:25:43,881 --> 00:25:48,640
and there were 1,000 mosquitoes per square inch.

414
00:25:48,641 --> 00:25:50,920
And in the latter part of August,

415
00:25:50,921 --> 00:25:53,960
you had the little gnats that the Indians called no-see-ums.

416
00:25:53,961 --> 00:25:56,800
They'd fly up your nose, in your ears.

417
00:25:56,801 --> 00:25:59,120
They'd put in our drovers, goofy.

418
00:25:59,121 --> 00:26:02,360
I mean, you'd be in an enclosed pup tent,

419
00:26:02,361 --> 00:26:05,000
and there was a regular hum outside.

420
00:26:07,040 --> 00:26:08,760
The months-long daily grind

421
00:26:08,761 --> 00:26:13,760
hours with no leave, being isolated from the outside world,

422
00:26:13,761 --> 00:26:18,760
battling Arctic conditions, then summer heat could wreak havoc

423
00:26:18,761 --> 00:26:22,760
on a soldier's psyche. There were several soldiers

424
00:26:22,761 --> 00:26:24,760
taken out of the Yukon in straitjackets.

425
00:26:24,761 --> 00:26:27,760
They were in the middle of nowhere doing, you know, doing

426
00:26:27,761 --> 00:26:30,760
their everyday work, and all of a sudden something just snapped.

427
00:26:30,761 --> 00:26:33,760
They just lost it. They couldn't cope.

428
00:26:33,761 --> 00:26:39,260
By July 1942, the project, which

429
00:26:39,261 --> 00:26:41,760
was scheduled to take up to two years to complete,

430
00:26:41,761 --> 00:26:46,400
was running smoothly. In about four months,

431
00:26:46,401 --> 00:26:49,500
more than 1,000 miles of road had been built.

432
00:26:52,440 --> 00:26:56,080
Overcoming their daily battles with permafrost and muskeg,

433
00:26:56,081 --> 00:27:00,580
each regiment was now building an average of 10 miles a day.

434
00:27:00,581 --> 00:27:03,580
The novice caterpillar operators, known as cat skinners,

435
00:27:03,581 --> 00:27:08,580
were now experts. And the construction was going

436
00:27:08,581 --> 00:27:12,580
so fast that the construction workers were living literally

437
00:27:12,581 --> 00:27:15,580
out of tents, and they were moving their camps on a weekly

438
00:27:15,581 --> 00:27:19,580
basis, so they never really got set up for proper housing and

439
00:27:19,581 --> 00:27:21,580
hygiene and all those kinds of things that we take for granted

440
00:27:21,581 --> 00:27:25,580
today. Even in early July,

441
00:27:25,581 --> 00:27:29,580
northern parts of the highway route were mired in cold weather.

442
00:27:29,581 --> 00:27:34,780
In their six-man tents, soldiers used World War I stoves

443
00:27:34,880 --> 00:27:36,080
to try to stay warm.

444
00:27:36,180 --> 00:27:41,120
And they were like an ice cream cone turned upside down.

445
00:27:41,220 --> 00:27:43,900
And they didn't have no bottom in them,

446
00:27:44,000 --> 00:27:47,700
just a stovepipe going out through the top of the tent.

447
00:27:47,800 --> 00:27:51,360
But you build a fire in the darn thing, and you get hot.

448
00:27:53,540 --> 00:27:56,300
Later that summer, as the weather warmed,

449
00:27:56,301 --> 00:28:00,300
completely discarded the tents and fought off the mosquitoes as

450
00:28:00,301 --> 00:28:05,300
best they could. The primitive camp settings

451
00:28:05,301 --> 00:28:08,300
were an open invitation to the wild animals that roamed the

452
00:28:08,301 --> 00:28:12,300
forests and bogs. Bears had no qualms about entering

453
00:28:12,301 --> 00:28:16,300
campsites looking for a quick bite.

454
00:28:16,301 --> 00:28:19,300
Naturally, wildlife is attracted by the smells of

455
00:28:19,301 --> 00:28:23,300
cooking and food, and so they would come into camps.

456
00:28:23,301 --> 00:28:26,800
And you got to remember, they had no natural fear of man then

457
00:28:26,801 --> 00:28:29,700
because they had never seen people before.

458
00:28:29,701 --> 00:28:34,440
So when soldiers started feeding scraps of food to the animals,

459
00:28:34,441 --> 00:28:37,640
and in some cases became a nuisance, some of them had to be

460
00:28:37,641 --> 00:28:42,520
put down, but others became pets.

461
00:28:42,521 --> 00:28:45,260
These adopted pets were a small diversion for the troops that

462
00:28:45,261 --> 00:28:48,760
had bled and toiled for so long.

463
00:28:48,761 --> 00:28:52,260
The push was on to finish the highway before the next winter

464
00:28:52,261 --> 00:28:55,260
hit.

465
00:28:55,360 --> 00:28:58,960
The 11,000 officers and soldiers racing to complete the Alcan

466
00:28:59,060 --> 00:29:04,700
Highway had overcome many obstacles since March 1942.

467
00:29:04,800 --> 00:29:07,200
By summer of that year, they had plowed through hundreds of

468
00:29:07,300 --> 00:29:11,420
thousands of trees and built hundreds of miles of road and

469
00:29:11,520 --> 00:29:15,620
nearly 100 bridges. In order to build bridges,

470
00:29:15,720 --> 00:29:19,760
makeshift sawmills were set up along river banks.

471
00:29:19,761 --> 00:29:24,460
We were sawing timbers that we needed to build bridges.

472
00:29:24,560 --> 00:29:26,960
We had horses up there to skid the timber

473
00:29:27,060 --> 00:29:30,600
because tractors would tend to sink in these places

474
00:29:30,700 --> 00:29:32,000
where we were cutting timber.

475
00:29:32,100 --> 00:29:35,680
And the cutting of timber was done by the fellas

476
00:29:35,780 --> 00:29:36,940
with crosscut saws.

477
00:29:39,620 --> 00:29:40,940
The troops had to build bridges

478
00:29:41,040 --> 00:29:45,080
across untamed streams, rivers, and lakes.

479
00:29:45,180 --> 00:29:48,260
Depending on the time of year, these seemingly innocent

480
00:29:48,261 --> 00:29:52,760
could turn into dangerous torrents within a few hours.

481
00:29:52,860 --> 00:29:55,300
Some of those rivers were wide some quarter mile.

482
00:29:55,400 --> 00:29:58,200
So it was quite an undertaking.

483
00:29:58,300 --> 00:30:02,800
We did have a few accidents where things would happen.

484
00:30:02,900 --> 00:30:06,620
I seen a man get knocked off a tractor by a spring pole.

485
00:30:06,720 --> 00:30:09,720
And the only way they got up to the hospital

486
00:30:09,820 --> 00:30:13,620
was on another tractor because there was no transportation.

487
00:30:13,720 --> 00:30:16,420
Not only was there no transportation,

488
00:30:16,421 --> 00:30:19,820
But the lack of Army requisitions forced the soldiers to stick to

489
00:30:19,821 --> 00:30:23,820
the basics. All of our work we'd done up

490
00:30:23,821 --> 00:30:29,860
there was done with logs and all we had was nails and most spikes.

491
00:30:29,861 --> 00:30:35,980
Everything was done with what nature supplied us.

492
00:30:35,981 --> 00:30:38,980
Ice forced the soldiers to use new construction methods to

493
00:30:38,981 --> 00:30:42,820
build bridges. Its presence just below riverbanks,

494
00:30:42,821 --> 00:30:46,320
where the pilings had to be driven prevented conventional

495
00:30:46,321 --> 00:30:49,120
methods from being used. On larger rivers,

496
00:30:49,121 --> 00:30:54,360
the ice present just beneath the riverbed was as hard as rock

497
00:30:54,361 --> 00:30:56,860
and fooled the troops into thinking they had sunken

498
00:30:56,861 --> 00:31:01,480
pilings into solid ground. But when warmer temperatures

499
00:31:01,481 --> 00:31:05,980
returned, the ice melted, causing bridge footings to slip.

500
00:31:08,480 --> 00:31:12,820
To combat this, the soldiers used steam hoses to melt the ice.

501
00:31:12,821 --> 00:31:16,420
The soldiers created holes for the pilings.

502
00:31:16,421 --> 00:31:19,820
The pilings were then greased, wrapped, and driven into the

503
00:31:19,821 --> 00:31:23,820
holes. The soil refroze around the

504
00:31:23,821 --> 00:31:27,820
driven, greased pilings, cementing them in place.

505
00:31:27,821 --> 00:31:33,820
Greasing the pilings prevents a phenomenon known as frost jacking.

506
00:31:33,821 --> 00:31:37,820
An ungreased piling inserted into frozen ground can heave once

507
00:31:37,821 --> 00:31:42,820
that ground starts to thaw. Repeated heaving can be

508
00:31:42,821 --> 00:31:46,820
the piling out of the ground. But even during summer thaws,

509
00:31:46,821 --> 00:31:50,820
the adhesive nature of the tar paper kept the piling secure.

510
00:31:50,821 --> 00:31:54,820
That provided a firm foundation for the bridge.

511
00:31:56,820 --> 00:32:00,820
Every regiment faced these kinds of obstacles.

512
00:32:00,821 --> 00:32:04,820
But it was the all-black 95th Unit that took bridge building

513
00:32:04,821 --> 00:32:08,820
to another level. The 95th was ordered to build

514
00:32:08,821 --> 00:32:12,820
a bridge over the Sickeny Chief River in just one week.

515
00:32:12,821 --> 00:32:17,820
The Sickeny is a swift-flowing river, nearly 300 feet wide

516
00:32:17,920 --> 00:32:20,420
at the point where the bridge was to be built.

517
00:32:20,520 --> 00:32:24,360
Most of the men in that unit bet their entire paychecks

518
00:32:24,460 --> 00:32:28,360
that they could build that bridge in record time.

519
00:32:28,460 --> 00:32:31,080
Working with 20 hours of sunlight,

520
00:32:31,180 --> 00:32:34,480
the 95th felled trees, squared timber,

521
00:32:34,580 --> 00:32:39,180
and waded into chest-high icy water to meet their goal.

522
00:32:39,280 --> 00:32:41,780
They used long ropes tied to the riverbank

523
00:32:41,781 --> 00:32:45,780
to keep from being swept downriver by the current.

524
00:32:45,781 --> 00:32:49,780
The 1,300 men sang work chants as they labored around the clock,

525
00:32:49,781 --> 00:32:54,780
finishing the bridge in an incredible 72 hours.

526
00:32:54,781 --> 00:32:56,780
Not only did they have the satisfaction

527
00:32:56,781 --> 00:32:58,780
of doing a magnificent piece of work,

528
00:32:58,781 --> 00:33:01,780
but they all scored financially.

529
00:33:01,781 --> 00:33:03,780
The seven black and white regiments

530
00:33:03,781 --> 00:33:06,780
that rarely saw each other and almost never interacted

531
00:33:06,781 --> 00:33:09,780
had battled isolation, the elements,

532
00:33:09,781 --> 00:33:12,420
and their own inexperience as bulldozer and tractor

533
00:33:12,520 --> 00:33:15,780
operators to build a serviceable roadway connecting

534
00:33:15,880 --> 00:33:22,260
the US to Alaska. The troops had done so well

535
00:33:22,360 --> 00:33:26,360
that by August of 1942, military officials in Washington,

536
00:33:26,460 --> 00:33:29,700
DC felt the highway could be completed before winter arrived.

537
00:33:33,000 --> 00:33:35,600
As implausible as it may have seemed a mere five months

538
00:33:35,700 --> 00:33:38,740
earlier, the troops were ahead of schedule.

539
00:33:38,741 --> 00:33:42,280
Suddenly, soldiers used to working 12 to 14 hours a day

540
00:33:42,380 --> 00:33:45,140
were now granted leave.

541
00:33:45,240 --> 00:33:49,080
Every regiment had its recreational officers

542
00:33:49,180 --> 00:33:52,680
that would try to liven up camp life,

543
00:33:52,780 --> 00:33:54,780
and they had portable record players.

544
00:33:54,880 --> 00:33:55,700
They improvised.

545
00:33:55,800 --> 00:33:57,100
They entertained each other.

546
00:33:57,200 --> 00:33:59,000
If there was a guitar around, that was it.

547
00:33:59,100 --> 00:34:01,100
The evening's entertainment would have been centered

548
00:34:01,200 --> 00:34:04,440
around any kind of music like that, and that's universal.

549
00:34:04,540 --> 00:34:08,140
The actives that took place were

550
00:34:08,141 --> 00:34:16,340
baseball, softball, and we had boxing.

551
00:34:16,440 --> 00:34:21,880
Perhaps the best known diversions for the soldiers were the signposts they created.

552
00:34:21,980 --> 00:34:29,200
The all-white 341st Regiment working in the Yukon and the all-black 97th Regiment working in Alaska

553
00:34:29,300 --> 00:34:33,740
built reminders of home that were equal parts humor and truth.

554
00:34:33,741 --> 00:34:37,900
Carl Lindley, a member of the 341st, started the Watson Lake

555
00:34:37,901 --> 00:34:41,380
Signpost Forest to battle his own homesickness.

556
00:34:41,381 --> 00:34:46,100
He had signs pointing to Tokyo, to New York City, to London,

557
00:34:46,101 --> 00:34:48,700
to a steakhouse somewhere in Chicago.

558
00:34:48,701 --> 00:34:51,100
It got to be famous for all those reasons,

559
00:34:51,101 --> 00:34:54,180
and then it just kept being added too.

560
00:34:54,181 --> 00:34:56,780
These brief but necessary respites gave the soldiers

561
00:34:56,781 --> 00:34:58,980
the second wind they needed to finish the job.

562
00:34:58,981 --> 00:35:05,380
By September 1942, the Southern section was finished.

563
00:35:05,381 --> 00:35:10,220
The All-White 35th Regiment met up with the 340th, another All-White

564
00:35:10,221 --> 00:35:14,420
Regiment, just east of Watson Lake, at a location renamed Contact

565
00:35:14,421 --> 00:35:18,940
Creek by the soldiers. Attention now shifted to the

566
00:35:18,941 --> 00:35:22,780
north, where a 100-mile gap between the All-White 18th

567
00:35:22,781 --> 00:35:26,780
Regiment and the All-Black 97th Regiment was the final hurdle to

568
00:35:26,781 --> 00:35:28,980
hurdle to completing the highway.

569
00:35:28,981 --> 00:35:33,320
So the 97th had a real incentive because their

570
00:35:33,321 --> 00:35:38,320
commander said, you can't go home until you build the road, and

571
00:35:38,321 --> 00:35:42,320
you'll march on that road south, and once you finally connect

572
00:35:42,321 --> 00:35:47,740
with Dawson Creek, you can get back on the train and go home.

573
00:35:47,741 --> 00:35:51,940
The 97th forged ahead, blazing trails south toward the

574
00:35:51,941 --> 00:35:55,580
all-white 18th Regiment. Instead of continuing to build

575
00:35:55,581 --> 00:36:00,540
to build a road wide enough for two military trucks, the 97th

576
00:36:00,541 --> 00:36:04,540
followed orders to build a simple one-way access trail that

577
00:36:04,541 --> 00:36:07,540
would be widened and improved shortly after the highway was

578
00:36:07,541 --> 00:36:14,540
opened. On October 25, 1942, with another

579
00:36:14,541 --> 00:36:19,540
Arctic winter fast approaching, the 97th crossed over into

580
00:36:19,541 --> 00:36:23,540
Canada and met the 18th near Beaver Creek at a location the

581
00:36:23,541 --> 00:36:27,780
the soldiers renamed Soldiers Summit.

582
00:36:27,781 --> 00:36:30,640
The road, carved through virgin territory under the worst

583
00:36:30,641 --> 00:36:34,480
conditions possible, was finished.

584
00:36:34,481 --> 00:36:36,880
Less than 100 soldiers died during the highway's

585
00:36:36,881 --> 00:36:41,300
construction, most from exposure.

586
00:36:41,301 --> 00:36:44,200
Several bulldozer operators died after huge branches fell

587
00:36:44,201 --> 00:36:48,300
on top of them. Despite these losses,

588
00:36:48,301 --> 00:36:52,700
a project estimated to take up to two years had been completed

589
00:36:52,701 --> 00:36:57,240
completed in just eight months and 12 days.

590
00:36:57,241 --> 00:37:01,800
I was most enthused because that meant going home.

591
00:37:01,801 --> 00:37:06,000
And everybody was overjoyed because the highway was

592
00:37:06,001 --> 00:37:06,800
completed.

593
00:37:06,801 --> 00:37:09,300
There were a lot of soldiers who didn't think they would get

594
00:37:09,301 --> 00:37:11,100
back home.

595
00:37:11,101 --> 00:37:16,940
But we made it, and thank God we made it.

596
00:37:16,941 --> 00:37:21,940
The official ceremony took place on November 20.

597
00:37:21,941 --> 00:37:26,740
from Alaska, Canada, and the US raved the minus 35 degree

598
00:37:26,741 --> 00:37:31,540
weather as military convoy trucks rolled down the highway.

599
00:37:36,580 --> 00:37:41,780
By December 1942, once the Pioneer Road was completed,

600
00:37:41,781 --> 00:37:44,340
the American and Canadian Civilian Public Roads

601
00:37:44,341 --> 00:37:50,580
Administration, otherwise known as PRA, took over.

602
00:37:50,581 --> 00:37:53,880
They would widen, straighten, and pave the road, transforming

603
00:37:53,881 --> 00:37:59,080
it from the Pioneer Road into the Alcan Highway.

604
00:37:59,081 --> 00:38:01,580
These improvements occurred as military convoys started

605
00:38:01,581 --> 00:38:04,080
delivering supplies to the airfields that made up the

606
00:38:04,081 --> 00:38:13,080
Northwest staging route. At dusk on October 13, 1943,

607
00:38:13,081 --> 00:38:16,580
workers from the PRA units touched bulldozer blades near

608
00:38:16,581 --> 00:38:21,380
Whitehorse, Yukon Territory, officially completing the task,

609
00:38:21,381 --> 00:38:29,620
which started in March 1942. Traffic along the highway was

610
00:38:29,621 --> 00:38:32,620
far lighter than expected because air or sea lanes to

611
00:38:32,621 --> 00:38:37,740
Alaska had not been cut off. The closest the Japanese got to

612
00:38:37,741 --> 00:38:45,540
invading Alaska was in June 1942, when the US Army killed more

613
00:38:45,541 --> 00:38:49,420
killed more than 2,000 Japanese soldiers during a battle in the

614
00:38:49,421 --> 00:38:53,580
Aleutian Islands. The highway opened for civilian

615
00:38:53,581 --> 00:38:59,580
traffic in 1948. In 1964, maintenance of the

616
00:38:59,581 --> 00:39:02,700
Canadian portion of the highway was transferred from the US Army

617
00:39:02,701 --> 00:39:10,700
to the PRA, now known as Public Works Canada.

618
00:39:10,701 --> 00:39:14,460
In the early 1990s, the highway was garnering attention as its

619
00:39:14,461 --> 00:39:17,960
anniversary approached. Reunions and celebrations were

620
00:39:17,961 --> 00:39:20,960
scheduled around the country to congratulate the soldiers for

621
00:39:20,961 --> 00:39:27,400
their efforts. Still, recognition for the black

622
00:39:27,401 --> 00:39:32,020
soldiers seemed in short supply. The fellows who wrote the

623
00:39:32,021 --> 00:39:36,020
history book were all white, and because the road had been a

624
00:39:36,021 --> 00:39:39,160
secret, and because the blacks had been way out in the middle

625
00:39:39,161 --> 00:39:43,120
of the Tule's and forbidden to go to the towns, nobody much

626
00:39:43,121 --> 00:39:45,720
Nobody much knew they were there.

627
00:39:45,721 --> 00:39:49,760
It was easy to write them out of history.

628
00:39:49,761 --> 00:39:52,560
We had great pride in what we were doing.

629
00:39:52,561 --> 00:39:57,060
And the thing that disturbed me is, over the years,

630
00:39:57,061 --> 00:39:59,440
the fact that no credit was given for the work

631
00:39:59,441 --> 00:40:01,640
that we actually did.

632
00:40:01,641 --> 00:40:03,840
And I wrote letters, and I tried everywhere

633
00:40:03,841 --> 00:40:06,340
to get more recognition for what we had accomplished.

634
00:40:06,341 --> 00:40:13,340
Finally, in 1992, at a Pentagon ceremony attended by then head

635
00:40:13,341 --> 00:40:16,340
of the Joint Chiefs of Staff Colin Powell, the black soldiers

636
00:40:16,341 --> 00:40:20,340
were officially recognized for their service.

637
00:40:20,341 --> 00:40:24,340
In 1996, another honor came the soldiers' way.

638
00:40:24,341 --> 00:40:27,340
The International Historical Engineering Landmark Commission

639
00:40:27,341 --> 00:40:31,340
cited the highway, now known as the Alaska Highway, as an

640
00:40:31,341 --> 00:40:33,340
international landmark.

641
00:40:33,341 --> 00:40:35,300
as an international landmark.

642
00:40:38,340 --> 00:40:40,380
But perhaps of even greater importance

643
00:40:40,480 --> 00:40:42,580
was how the highway changed the perception

644
00:40:42,680 --> 00:40:46,080
of black soldiers and black people in general.

645
00:40:46,180 --> 00:40:49,280
There were 10,600 men building that highway.

646
00:40:49,380 --> 00:40:51,500
One third of them were black.

647
00:40:51,600 --> 00:40:54,000
And nobody had to tell them they were doing a good job

648
00:40:54,100 --> 00:40:57,100
or that they could do just as good a job as anybody else.

649
00:40:57,200 --> 00:40:58,700
They could see it.

650
00:40:58,800 --> 00:41:01,800
And when you know that, nobody's going to stop you.

651
00:41:01,801 --> 00:41:06,200
stop you. I think that's what made America

652
00:41:06,201 --> 00:41:13,760
become a more integrated country. Today, the Alaska Highway is a hot

653
00:41:13,761 --> 00:41:18,300
spot for tourists. The area's natural beauty and

654
00:41:18,301 --> 00:41:23,880
winding roads bring in visitors from as far away as Europe and

655
00:41:23,881 --> 00:41:26,440
Asia. Decades of constant

656
00:41:26,441 --> 00:41:29,960
straightening by PRA units have shortened the highway.

657
00:41:29,961 --> 00:41:33,400
Originally more than 1,600 miles long.

658
00:41:33,500 --> 00:41:38,360
Today, it's roughly 1,500 miles.

659
00:41:38,460 --> 00:41:41,300
The government of Canada spends about $25 million a year

660
00:41:41,400 --> 00:41:42,400
on the highway.

661
00:41:42,500 --> 00:41:44,700
Half of that is operations and maintenance.

662
00:41:44,800 --> 00:41:47,320
And the other half is improvements and upgrades

663
00:41:47,420 --> 00:41:48,820
to the highway.

664
00:41:48,920 --> 00:41:51,820
The region, in parts at least, is very remote.

665
00:41:51,920 --> 00:41:53,320
You're far from the kinds of services

666
00:41:53,420 --> 00:41:56,120
you need to maintain a road.

667
00:41:56,220 --> 00:41:58,120
A lot of the highway is still original.

668
00:41:58,121 --> 00:42:01,120
That means it hasn't been upgraded yet to modern asphalt.

669
00:42:04,120 --> 00:42:07,620
There are initial discussions to extend the highway to Nome,

670
00:42:07,621 --> 00:42:11,120
and perhaps even build a bridge from Alaska to Siberia.

671
00:42:11,121 --> 00:42:14,120
That would certainly continue in the tradition of the original

672
00:42:14,121 --> 00:42:21,120
Alcan Highway, making the nearly impossible a reality.

673
00:42:21,121 --> 00:42:25,120
As an engineer, the highway is a phenomenal feat.

674
00:42:25,121 --> 00:42:31,116
phenomenal feat. If you think about it, 11,000 men were moved into the area.

675
00:42:31,117 --> 00:42:32,220
They pioneered

676
00:42:32,221 --> 00:42:40,660
a road in eight months, and the simple logistics of building the road were just mind-boggling.

677
00:42:40,661 --> 00:42:47,160
And they were basically told to go from point A to point B, and the men just simply found

678
00:42:47,161 --> 00:42:48,952
the best way that they could get there.

679
00:42:48,953 --> 00:42:51,020
I don't even think we could take on a project

680
00:42:51,021 --> 00:42:53,020
that size and do it that quickly today.

681
00:42:55,520 --> 00:42:58,220
I've grown from that experience in Alaska.

682
00:43:00,420 --> 00:43:03,260
It broadened my knowledge and it had moved me

683
00:43:03,560 --> 00:43:05,860
to right where I am today.

684
00:43:08,680 --> 00:43:12,480
I wouldn't take a million dollars for the experience that I had,

685
00:43:13,380 --> 00:43:16,020
but I sure wouldn't give a red cent to go through it again.

686
00:43:17,220 --> 00:43:19,980
No sirree. I wouldn't want to do that again.

